This page contains no mathematical derivation. It is a page of methodological and aphoristic notes from Hilbert's notebook, with four parts.

(1) Lines 1-3 finish an outline of physics begun at the bottom of the previous/next-page sequence: '1.) Grundlegende Differentialgl. (a. Continuum-Methode, b. Atomen-Methode)'. This page continues with '2.) Zubereitung der Differentialgl.: a. Specialisiren der Grundgl., b. Grenzfälle (verschwindende Dimensionen, Stösse, Unstetigkeit)' and '3.) Integration der Differentialgl.: a. gewöhnliche, b. partielle'. In modern terms the scheme is:
- formulate the fundamental field or particle equations;
- reduce them by specialisation and limiting cases (thin bodies or lower-dimensional limits, impact or collision, discontinuities);
- solve the resulting ODEs and PDEs.

(2) The '3+1=4' note is a satirical aside on inflated terminology, not a theorem. Hilbert first wrote something like '3 (crossed symbol) 1' and then inserted '+1'. He then proposes calling 3 the 'Index', 1 the 'Charakteristik' and 4 the 'Excess'. One then has 'den schönen Satz à la Sylvester, dass Index + Charakteristik stets gleich dem Excess ist, oder so ähnlich'. The reading 'Charakteristik' and 'stets' is my interpretation of the abbreviations. The joke mocks Sylvester's habit of coining names: index, signature, characteristic, excess, as in the law of inertia of quadratic forms and Sylvester's many neologisms. Dressing a triviality (3+1=4) in such terms makes it look like a deep law. Mathematically, the content is only the arithmetic identity 3+1=4.

(3) This part restates the previous page's remark on axiomatics. One can 'skip the axioms at one stroke':
- in geometry, by demanding that every line be representable by a linear equation (i.e., by postulating coordinate/analytic geometry);
- in mechanics, by postulating Hamilton's principle immediately;
- in thermodynamics, by postulating the entropy function and its equation.

The ellipsis 'Aber gerade diese Zerlegung in Axiome [ist nötig/wertvoll]' is grammatically incomplete; the verb is my supplement. The reasons given are:
- otherwise it is not at all clear what is contained in the general principle;
- otherwise, the 'curse of mathematics', everything degenerates into and is overgrown by the purely formal.

This is Hilbert's programme of axiomatizing geometry and physics, as in Grundlagen der Geometrie (1899) and the 6th Paris problem (1900), in embryonic form.

(4) A remark on the division of labour in science. If one person is only a synthetic geometer, another only a theorist of quadratic forms, and another only a 'pathological' function theorist, it would be like a human community in which one only eats (or walks), another only sleeps or drinks, and a third only works. Each would then wither ('Jeder müsste dann verkümmern'). This plea for the unity of mathematics is a known Hilbert theme, cf. the 1900 Paris address on the unity of mathematics.
